---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-copd`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and COPD',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'J44', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and COPD', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and COPD: Exacerbations, ViDiCO Trial, and Deficiency Correction"
  description="Vitamin D deficiency correlates with COPD exacerbation frequency. What ViDiCO and Cochrane show for supplementation — the effect is largest in the deficient."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-copd", label: "Vitamin D & COPD" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and COPD</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease patients have high rates of vitamin D
      deficiency — over half in most cohorts. Reduced sun exposure, low BMI (or the
      opposite: obesity), chronic corticosteroid use, and reduced mobility all
      contribute. Deficiency correlates with more frequent exacerbations.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The ViDiCO trial</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The 240-patient ViDiCO trial (Martineau 2015) gave 120,000 IU cholecalciferol every 2
      months vs placebo to COPD patients. Overall: no significant reduction in moderate-to-
      severe exacerbations. But pre-specified subgroup analysis showed a 45% reduction in
      exacerbations in patients with baseline 25(OH)D &lt; 20 ng/mL — the same pattern seen
      in asthma trials.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cochrane meta-analysis</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A 2019 Cochrane review pooling 4 trials found a 43% reduction in exacerbation rate
      in adults with baseline 25(OH)D &lt; 25 nmol/L (&lt; 10 ng/mL). No benefit in
      vitamin D-replete patients. The evidence supports correcting deficiency, not
      supplementing indiscriminately.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Mechanism</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cathelicidin (LL-37) is a vitamin D-inducible antimicrobial peptide that helps
      clear the respiratory pathogens (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae,
      viruses) that trigger COPD exacerbations. Vitamin D also downregulates airway
      inflammation and remodelling pathways.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical dosing</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Test 25(OH)D in all COPD patients, especially those with ≥ 2 exacerbations per year.</li>
      <li>If &lt; 20 ng/mL: 2,000–4,000 IU/day for 8–12 weeks, then re-test.</li>
      <li>Target maintenance 30–50 ng/mL.</li>
      <li>Chronic inhaled or oral corticosteroids also warrant DEXA and osteoporosis workup — vitamin D + calcium is standard.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Vitamin D is adjunctive — bronchodilators,
      inhaled steroids for eosinophilic COPD, smoking cessation, pulmonary rehab, and
      vaccination are the evidence-based interventions that change the disease course.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-copd" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
